WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.451 --> 00:00:02.650
- She's an American technology executive,

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.301
activist and author.

00:00:04.301 --> 00:00:06.926
She's the chief operating
officer of Facebook.

00:00:06.926 --> 00:00:08.523
Her estimated net worth

00:00:08.523 --> 00:00:10.945
is reported to be over
one billion dollars.

00:00:10.945 --> 00:00:13.045
She's Sheryl Sandberg, and here are her

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:15.212
top ten rules for success.

00:00:18.305 --> 00:00:20.526
- And so I thought about
what I wanted to say.

00:00:20.526 --> 00:00:22.374
What would I pass on if you were trying

00:00:22.374 --> 00:00:24.982
to be an entrepreneur, if
you're trying to be a leader.

00:00:24.982 --> 00:00:26.807
I think if you're trying
to be those things,

00:00:26.807 --> 00:00:29.171
what you're really trying to
do on a fundamental level,

00:00:29.171 --> 00:00:30.599
is have impact.

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:31.741
You're trying to have impact.

00:00:31.741 --> 00:00:33.955
You're trying to have the things you do,

00:00:33.955 --> 00:00:36.720
the things that you spend your time doing,

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.010
have impact, meaning change
something around the world.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:42.669
And for me, I think the
lesson is really simple,

00:00:42.669 --> 00:00:46.223
and it's particularly important
in the era in which we live,

00:00:46.223 --> 00:00:48.499
and for me the industry in which I work,

00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:51.265
which is that this is about scale.

00:00:51.265 --> 00:00:52.332
It's all about scale.

00:00:52.332 --> 00:00:56.294
Having impact is all about how
you do something that scales.

00:00:56.294 --> 00:00:59.166
And by scale I mean things
that can have broad impact

00:00:59.166 --> 00:01:02.482
potentially things that have
decreasing marginal cost.

00:01:02.482 --> 00:01:05.053
Things that can scale beyond
the one-to-one interaction

00:01:05.053 --> 00:01:06.683
you have as a person.

00:01:06.683 --> 00:01:08.443
I think the most important
thing about growing a business

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:10.397
successfully, is thinking ahead,

00:01:10.397 --> 00:01:12.653
about where you're going to be.

00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:14.369
So I think about the three tips

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:16.660
you have for scaling your organization.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.864
The first and I think the most
important is to think big.

00:01:19.864 --> 00:01:21.520
People think about how do you manage,

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.379
how do you motivate an organization.

00:01:24.379 --> 00:01:26.605
And they think about management,

00:01:26.605 --> 00:01:28.699
basically the science of
administering a business,

00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:29.532
or leadership.

00:01:29.532 --> 00:01:31.572
And my favorite definition of leadership

00:01:31.572 --> 00:01:34.091
is the art of accomplishing
more than the science

00:01:34.091 --> 00:01:36.458
of management says is possible.

00:01:36.458 --> 00:01:39.595
When you have a big vision,
when you're thinking really big,

00:01:39.595 --> 00:01:42.505
something that can change the
world, so, my favorite example

00:01:42.505 --> 00:01:43.971
connecting the world,

00:01:43.971 --> 00:01:46.614
which was Mark Zuckerberg's
vision for Facebook,

00:01:46.614 --> 00:01:48.377
that's the kind of thing
that excites people

00:01:48.377 --> 00:01:49.625
and motivates them.

00:01:49.625 --> 00:01:51.515
That gets them to follow you,

00:01:51.515 --> 00:01:53.237
not just because they work for you

00:01:53.237 --> 00:01:55.072
and they need to do what you say,

00:01:55.072 --> 00:01:58.315
but because they believe in
what you're trying to do.

00:01:58.315 --> 00:02:00.522
And when you're trying to
really scale an organization,

00:02:00.522 --> 00:02:03.532
you need to go really
quickly and get people

00:02:03.532 --> 00:02:07.105
to accomplish more than
they thought was possible.

00:02:07.105 --> 00:02:10.308
And I think that comes
from internal motivation,

00:02:10.308 --> 00:02:13.578
which really comes from a
vision that's big enough.

00:02:13.578 --> 00:02:15.656
I think the most common
mistake I see people make,

00:02:15.656 --> 00:02:18.468
and men make this mistake
too, but women do a lot,

00:02:18.468 --> 00:02:21.549
is they're too worried
about the upward trajectory,

00:02:21.549 --> 00:02:23.327
and not worried enough about growth.

00:02:23.327 --> 00:02:25.534
So, the two corporate jobs I've taken,

00:02:25.534 --> 00:02:27.337
I went from Treasury to Google,

00:02:27.337 --> 00:02:29.257
and then I went from Google to Facebook,

00:02:29.257 --> 00:02:31.028
had one thing in common which is that

00:02:31.028 --> 00:02:34.512
they were more junior, other
people's words, not mine,

00:02:34.512 --> 00:02:36.795
than other jobs I was offered.

00:02:36.795 --> 00:02:38.997
When I went to Google,
Google was like 250 people,

00:02:38.997 --> 00:02:41.865
I was going to be a business
unit general manager,

00:02:41.865 --> 00:02:43.379
except there were no business units.

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:45.616
Susan Wajusky's here,
she helped recruit me.

00:02:45.616 --> 00:02:47.824
This was the non-job of all time.

00:02:47.824 --> 00:02:49.469
I mean there was no job there.

00:02:49.469 --> 00:02:53.605
And I was offered senior
sounding, more senior roles

00:02:53.605 --> 00:02:55.999
at more established companies.

00:02:55.999 --> 00:02:57.626
But I really believed
in what Google was doing

00:02:57.626 --> 00:02:59.782
and Eric Schmidt gave
me great career advice,

00:02:59.782 --> 00:03:01.365
he said, go for growth.

00:03:01.365 --> 00:03:02.811
Growth moves everyone up.

00:03:02.811 --> 00:03:04.103
If it's growing it works.

00:03:04.103 --> 00:03:06.987
And so, I went to a smaller company,

00:03:06.987 --> 00:03:10.570
with a very, more
junior, less-defined job.

00:03:11.527 --> 00:03:13.875
When I came out of Google,
it seems obvious now,

00:03:13.875 --> 00:03:15.458
to come to Facebook, but at the time,

00:03:15.458 --> 00:03:16.904
a lot of people asked me,
right, you remember this,

00:03:16.904 --> 00:03:18.404
what are ya doin'?

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:23.556
You're going to work for
a 23-year old, you know.

00:03:23.556 --> 00:03:25.803
No one knows if Facebook's
going to be the next MySpace

00:03:25.803 --> 00:03:29.128
or Friendster, and you're not CEO.

00:03:29.128 --> 00:03:30.393
You could be CEO somewhere else.

00:03:30.393 --> 00:03:32.909
And what I saw was
something that mattered.

00:03:32.909 --> 00:03:35.399
Facebook mattered, with
authentic identity,

00:03:35.399 --> 00:03:37.139
and an opportunity for growth.

00:03:37.139 --> 00:03:40.964
And so at each stage, I
cared less about my level,

00:03:40.964 --> 00:03:42.480
I kind of think it's silly,

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.435
than I have about the underlying growth.

00:03:44.435 --> 00:03:46.711
And I do think this is really important

00:03:46.711 --> 00:03:48.185
for everyone to know.

00:03:48.185 --> 00:03:50.493
How do you communicate authentically?

00:03:50.493 --> 00:03:53.263
How do you figure out what
to say and what not to say

00:03:53.263 --> 00:03:55.214
in a way that's authentic?

00:03:55.214 --> 00:03:57.736
And what Fred says, and I
really believe this is true,

00:03:57.736 --> 00:04:00.551
is it starts from the
fundamental understanding

00:04:00.551 --> 00:04:02.527
that there is no truth.

00:04:02.527 --> 00:04:04.220
There's my truth, there's your truth

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:05.676
and everything is subjective.

00:04:05.676 --> 00:04:07.317
And so if you always
start from the position of

00:04:07.317 --> 00:04:10.437
this is what I believe, I
don't expect you to believe it,

00:04:10.437 --> 00:04:11.742
I don't think you have to believe it,

00:04:11.742 --> 00:04:13.613
I'm not saying it's true,

00:04:13.613 --> 00:04:16.187
you can actually always
communicate authentically.

00:04:16.187 --> 00:04:17.371
Because if you walk in the room,

00:04:17.371 --> 00:04:19.057
and this gets worse as
you get more senior,

00:04:19.057 --> 00:04:21.720
here's the answer, you're
not giving anyone else

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.542
any room to say anything.

00:04:23.542 --> 00:04:25.205
And if you walk in the room and say,

00:04:25.205 --> 00:04:27.897
I believe this for this reason.

00:04:27.897 --> 00:04:29.887
What do you believe?

00:04:29.887 --> 00:04:33.370
If you share your truth in that language,

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:35.756
you give people room to authenticate,

00:04:35.756 --> 00:04:37.814
to communicate authentically.

00:04:37.814 --> 00:04:40.239
And that is hugely important
to these relationships

00:04:40.239 --> 00:04:41.822
at any stage.

00:04:41.822 --> 00:04:44.483
I think the second thing
is you have to hire big.

00:04:44.483 --> 00:04:48.433
And big, I don't mean famous,
I mean ahead of where you are.

00:04:48.433 --> 00:04:51.611
So when you're growing an
organization really quickly

00:04:51.611 --> 00:04:54.766
what happens is that what
normally takes an organization

00:04:54.766 --> 00:04:57.865
years, happens in a period of just months.

00:04:57.865 --> 00:05:01.090
So, when I was at Google,
my team went from four

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:03.507
to 4,000 in about five years.

00:05:05.306 --> 00:05:07.285
That means that if you look
at those first four people

00:05:07.285 --> 00:05:09.972
I hired, in order for them
to be part of the people

00:05:09.972 --> 00:05:12.675
who were running that organization,

00:05:12.675 --> 00:05:17.045
they need to do about 10 or
15 years worth of growing

00:05:17.045 --> 00:05:21.355
and getting experience, in a
much shorter period of time.

00:05:21.355 --> 00:05:22.914
So if you think you're
going to grow quickly,

00:05:22.914 --> 00:05:25.101
hire for what you think
you're going to need.

00:05:25.101 --> 00:05:26.180
Over hire.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:29.436
Hire people who are more
qualified, have more experience,

00:05:29.436 --> 00:05:32.050
or hire people who are
right out of school,

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:35.613
but can overachieve in
their current roles.

00:05:35.613 --> 00:05:37.060
I don't think it's the number
of years in the workforce

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:39.867
that matters, I think
it's hiring the people

00:05:39.867 --> 00:05:43.514
that you're going to need
then, now, because then

00:05:43.514 --> 00:05:45.317
happens so quickly.

00:05:45.317 --> 00:05:48.630
I really believe we lead best,
when we walk side by side

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:50.314
with our colleagues.

00:05:50.314 --> 00:05:52.957
And to achieve this, you're
going to have to do the opposite

00:05:52.957 --> 00:05:54.778
of what I'm doing right now.

00:05:54.778 --> 00:05:58.257
You're going to have to not
just talk, but really listen.

00:05:58.257 --> 00:06:01.636
And you have to one step further,
which is not just listen,

00:06:01.636 --> 00:06:06.376
but find a way to get people
to actually tell you the truth.

00:06:06.376 --> 00:06:10.853
There is no such thing as
complete control, nothing.

00:06:10.853 --> 00:06:12.932
No one has complete
control in any situation.

00:06:12.932 --> 00:06:15.831
People that leading organizations
in some ways have less,

00:06:15.831 --> 00:06:18.036
because not only do they
have to control what they do

00:06:18.036 --> 00:06:22.203
they have to persuade
everyone else, what they do.

00:06:23.976 --> 00:06:28.244
But, if you are able
to take responsibility,

00:06:28.244 --> 00:06:30.709
I'm not late because there was traffic,

00:06:30.709 --> 00:06:33.042
I'm late because I
didn't leave early enough

00:06:33.042 --> 00:06:35.715
to account for the fact
that there was traffic.

00:06:35.715 --> 00:06:38.031
The project didn't get
finished not because my friend,

00:06:38.031 --> 00:06:41.497
my partner didn't do it, my
colleague didn't do is part.

00:06:41.497 --> 00:06:44.756
The project didn't get finished
because I didn't set up

00:06:44.756 --> 00:06:48.335
a team where my colleague
wanted to do his part.

00:06:48.335 --> 00:06:50.452
When you take responsibility

00:06:50.452 --> 00:06:52.105
and you take full responsibility,

00:06:52.105 --> 00:06:53.833
that is the most empowering thing

00:06:53.833 --> 00:06:55.813
and you can do it any stage.

00:06:55.813 --> 00:06:57.480
You have to do it if
you're raising money as an

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.648
entrepreneur, you have to
do it if you're trying to

00:07:00.648 --> 00:07:02.160
persuade people to work with you,

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:04.338
you have to do it all stages.

00:07:04.338 --> 00:07:05.965
One of things I talk about in my book

00:07:05.965 --> 00:07:07.972
is the importance of measuring results,

00:07:07.972 --> 00:07:09.854
not facetime.

00:07:09.854 --> 00:07:13.023
If you as a company measure
facetime, you will be rewarded

00:07:13.023 --> 00:07:14.190
with facetime.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.667
If you talk about the people
who are working really hard,

00:07:17.667 --> 00:07:19.683
rather than the people who
are getting great results,

00:07:19.683 --> 00:07:22.871
people will work very hard
but not focus on results.

00:07:22.871 --> 00:07:25.256
In my job at Facebook, Mark
and I try to run the company

00:07:25.256 --> 00:07:27.354
very focused on results.

00:07:27.354 --> 00:07:29.473
We had one employee, actually for a while,

00:07:29.473 --> 00:07:32.402
who famously was one of our
absolutely highest performers,

00:07:32.402 --> 00:07:35.738
but just didn't like to be in
the office very much at all.

00:07:35.738 --> 00:07:38.073
And we would publicly applaud him.

00:07:38.073 --> 00:07:40.200
No one's seen Shemoth in a week,

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.300
but look what's happening.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:45.826
And we were publicly saying
his results were amazing.

00:07:45.826 --> 00:07:46.953
If he feels like doing it,

00:07:46.953 --> 00:07:49.495
sitting in his basement, go ahead.

00:07:49.495 --> 00:07:51.032
Now, not every company can do that,

00:07:51.032 --> 00:07:53.102
different companies have
different constraints,

00:07:53.102 --> 00:07:55.705
but many more companies can do this.

00:07:55.705 --> 00:07:58.913
The best kind of leadership
starts by finding

00:07:58.913 --> 00:08:01.640
something you really believe in.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.870
Facebook exists because
Mark Zuckerberg believed

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:07.489
that the world would be a
better place if we all used

00:08:07.489 --> 00:08:09.848
technology to share and connect.

00:08:09.848 --> 00:08:13.405
He believed in that enough
to drop out of school at 19,

00:08:13.405 --> 00:08:16.224
and he believed in it enough
to hang onto that vision

00:08:16.224 --> 00:08:19.382
and hold onto it, even when
people tried to take it away

00:08:19.382 --> 00:08:21.688
from him over the years.

00:08:21.688 --> 00:08:24.637
I joined Facebook, along
with so many others,

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:27.032
because I too believe in that vision.

00:08:27.032 --> 00:08:29.537
And now it's the most
important part of my job,

00:08:29.537 --> 00:08:32.121
to keep myself and
everyone else at Facebook

00:08:32.121 --> 00:08:35.435
maniacally focused on
what we are trying to do,

00:08:35.435 --> 00:08:37.018
day in and day out.

00:08:39.355 --> 00:08:43.188
So start, start by finding
a company you believe in,

00:08:43.188 --> 00:08:47.095
a product you love,
something, a cause you really,

00:08:47.095 --> 00:08:48.937
really care about.

00:08:48.937 --> 00:08:51.955
Because not only will
you inspire yourself,

00:08:51.955 --> 00:08:56.282
will you be inspired, but you
in turn will inspire others.

00:08:56.282 --> 00:08:59.962
And that's the most critical
part of great leadership.

00:08:59.962 --> 00:09:03.082
All organizations have
some form of hierarchy.

00:09:03.082 --> 00:09:05.496
People in organizations,
if you're their manager,

00:09:05.496 --> 00:09:07.685
will do what you tell them to do,

00:09:07.685 --> 00:09:10.930
even at Facebook, at
least some of the time.

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:13.094
But great leaders don't want compliance,

00:09:13.094 --> 00:09:15.344
they don't want people to follow orders.

00:09:15.344 --> 00:09:18.580
Great leaders want real
excitement, genuine enthusiasm,

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:19.913
real commitment.

00:09:20.967 --> 00:09:23.790
Great leaders don't just
win the minds of their team,

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:25.718
they win their hearts.

00:09:25.718 --> 00:09:28.281
Great leaders don't just issue commands,

00:09:28.281 --> 00:09:30.905
they heed the voices of those around them.

00:09:30.905 --> 00:09:33.875
Careers are not ladders,
those days are long gone,

00:09:33.875 --> 00:09:35.622
but jungle gyms.

00:09:35.622 --> 00:09:36.832
Don't just move up and down.

00:09:36.832 --> 00:09:39.545
Don't just look up, look
backwards, sideways,

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:41.177
around corners.

00:09:41.177 --> 00:09:44.286
Your career and your life
will have starts and stops,

00:09:44.286 --> 00:09:45.880
and zigs and zags.

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:48.157
Don't stress out about the white space,

00:09:48.157 --> 00:09:50.899
the path you can't draw,
because therein lies

00:09:50.899 --> 00:09:54.324
both the surprises and the opportunities.

00:09:54.324 --> 00:09:56.336
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:09:56.336 --> 00:09:58.115
I made this video because
Veronica Watts asked me to,

00:09:58.115 --> 00:10:00.401
so if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:10:00.401 --> 00:10:02.045
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:10:02.045 --> 00:10:04.262
and I'll see what I can do.

00:10:04.262 --> 00:10:06.903
I'd also love to know which
of Sheryl's top ten rules

00:10:06.903 --> 00:10:08.625
meant the most to you.

00:10:08.625 --> 00:10:09.685
Leave it in the comments.

00:10:09.685 --> 00:10:11.209
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:10:11.209 --> 00:10:12.339
Thank you so much for watching,

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:13.673
you need to believe,

00:10:13.673 --> 00:10:15.564
and I'll see you soon.

00:10:15.564 --> 00:10:18.118
She's an American
technology executive, actor,

00:10:18.118 --> 00:10:19.752
no not actor, activist.

00:10:19.752 --> 00:10:20.762
She is not an actor.

00:10:20.762 --> 00:10:22.564
She is not an actor.

00:10:22.564 --> 00:10:24.981
She's an American technology,

